# Breaking changes to the standard library's public surface, declared.
#
# `scripts/check-compat.sh` refuses a removed, retyped or
# effect-widened public name unless a line here names it against the
# version that makes the change. That is the whole enforcement: a
# break is allowed, and it is allowed only when somebody wrote down
# that they meant it.
#
# Format, whitespace-separated:
#
#   <version>  <kind-letter>  <name>  <why>
#
# The kind letter is AXSYM's: F function, D data, C constructor,
# S struct, T trait. `docs/compatibility.md` is the policy this file
# implements.

# 0.3.3 - ERR-ADOPT-1 slice 3, the half of it where `-errno` genuinely
# means a failure. Seven filesystem calls and `copyFile` answer
# `(Result Int Error)` instead of an Int that is a byte count when
# positive and a negated errno when negative. See docs/error-model.md
# section 10.
0.3.3  F  writeFile   -errno -> (Result Int Error): the failure is in the type
0.3.3  F  appendFile  -errno -> (Result Int Error): the failure is in the type
0.3.3  F  removeFile  -errno -> (Result Int Error): the failure is in the type
0.3.3  F  renamePath  -errno -> (Result Int Error): the failure is in the type
0.3.3  F  fileSize    -errno -> (Result Int Error): the failure is in the type
0.3.3  F  makeDirAll  -errno -> (Result Int Error): the failure is in the type
0.3.3  F  removeDir   -errno -> (Result Int Error): the failure is in the type
0.3.3  F  copyFile    -errno -> (Result Int Error): the failure is in the type

# 0.3.4 - ERR-ADOPT-1 slice 3, the `Sys` half. Seven raw syscall
# wrappers answer `(Result Int Error)` instead of an Int that is a
# result when non-negative and a negated errno when negative.
#
# They read WIDENED rather than CHANGED because the effect row moved
# too: building an `Error` allocates, so each gained `Alloc`. The type
# changed as well - a widened row and a changed signature are the same
# event here, and the gate reports the coarser one.
0.3.4  F  sysWriteFile   -errno -> (Result Int Error)
0.3.4  F  sysAppendFile  -errno -> (Result Int Error)
0.3.4  F  sysUnlink      -errno -> (Result Int Error)
0.3.4  F  sysMkdir       -errno -> (Result Int Error)
0.3.4  F  sysRmdir       -errno -> (Result Int Error)
0.3.4  F  sysRename      -errno -> (Result Int Error)
0.3.4  F  sysFileSize    -errno -> (Result Int Error)
# `makeDir` went with them: its body is one `sysMkdir` call, and its
# declared `Int` return silently became a Result HANDLE - which the
# checker cannot catch, because `Int` is the universal heap-handle
# type. It printed a heap address as an errno until the fixture caught
# it.
0.3.4  F  makeDir        -errno -> (Result Int Error)
# `ioResult` now RE-WRAPS a `Sys` Result rather than converting a raw
# Int, because `Sys` converts at its own layer now.
0.3.4  F  ioResult       (Int -> ...) -> ((Result Int Error) -> ...)

# 0.3.4 - the process half of slice 3. `sysRun`'s three-way answer is
# now split by the TYPE rather than by a sign: `Err` means the child
# NEVER RAN (the spawn's own errno), `Ok` means it ran and carries what
# it answered, including 128+n for a signal. That is the distinction
# `Sys.ax`'s own comment says a driver must not lose - "could not start
# llc" against "llc rejected the module".
#
# The net calls are deliberately NOT here: `netPollWait` runs inside
# the echo server's event loop, where an `(Ok n)` block would allocate
# per poll wake outside the per-connection arena scope, and
# `check-net.sh` asserts memory ratios on exactly that server. Same
# reasoning that leaves `IO.writeStr` on a sentinel.
0.3.4  F  sysSpawn        -errno -> (Result Int Error): Err means no child
0.3.4  F  sysWaitPid      -errno -> (Result Int Error)
0.3.4  F  sysRun          -errno -> (Result Int Error): Err means the child never ran
0.3.4  F  sysRunPath      -errno -> (Result Int Error)
0.3.4  F  sysRandomBytes  -errno -> (Result Int Error)

# 0.6.0 - traits and `impl` were removed from the language. An
# interface is a CAPABILITY RECORD now: a parameterised struct holding
# the functions, bound with `fn` and passed as a value. `show` needs
# none of it - `showResolve` in the checker renders `String`, `Bool`,
# `Int`, `Float`, `Char` and every `data`/`struct` from the argument's
# static type, and always did, so the four `impl` blocks in
# `stdlib/Show.ax` were dead weight the day they were written.
#
# WHAT IS ACTUALLY LOST, stated rather than left to be discovered: a
# user could OVERRIDE the built-in rendering for their own type with
# `(impl (Show Color) ...)`. There is no override hook now.
# `deriveShow` still generates `showColor` as a plain function, so the
# deliberate case is covered by calling it; what is gone is silently
# reusing the `show` head for it.
0.6.0  T  Show  trait removed: an interface is a capability record, and `show` never needed one

# 0.6.0 - dogfooding parameterised structs (landed 2026-08-31): a
# `data` type with one constructor that existed only to carry a single
# function field is now the struct that field always was. The
# constructor name `HttpFn` is gone; construct with `(HttpHandler
# (lambda (fd r) ...))` and read the field with `.run` instead of
# matching. The compare tool keys identity as (kind, name), so a `D`
# becoming an `S` reads as this name REMOVED from the `D` row - the
# `S HttpHandler` row that replaces it is an ADDED row, which needs no
# declaration of its own.
0.6.0  D  HttpHandler  data HttpHandler (ctor HttpFn) -> struct HttpHandler (field run): a one-constructor data type is a struct

# 0.6.0 - dogfooding `Option`: four sentinel functions in
# `compat/SENTINELS`'s `absence` column answer `Option Int` in place
# of a raw `-1`, per `docs/error-model.md` ERR-REC-3 ("Option is for
# absence"). Zero external callers of any of the four, checked before
# porting; every caller that needed updating lived in the same file.
0.6.0  F  axsymHexVal    -1 -> (Option Int): absence, not a sentinel; no external callers
0.6.0  F  axsymPctAt     -1 -> (Option Int): absence, not a sentinel; no external callers
0.6.0  F  pathLastSlash  -1 -> (Option Int): absence, not a sentinel; no external callers
0.6.0  F  pathExtIndex   -1 -> (Option Int): absence, not a sentinel; no external callers

# 0.6.0 - the effect row learned that a constructor allocates
# (2026-08-31). NOT ONE LINE OF ANY FUNCTION BELOW CHANGED. What
# changed is the analysis: applying a `data` or `struct` constructor
# contributed nothing to the inferred effect row, by a decision
# `docs/memory-model.md` MM-EXEC-9a recorded and `ERR-PROP-2` relied
# on, and every function here answers an `Option` or a `Result` and
# has always allocated to do it.
#
# The decision was not survivable once `restrict(no-alloc)` existed to
# read the row: `no-alloc` is a CLAIM the compiler answers, and against
# a row built to omit allocation it answered OK to a body whose only
# act was allocating. So the row is precise now, and these 19 public
# names gained `Alloc`.
#
# THE HONEST READING OF THIS BLOCK, which is not 0.3.4's: a consumer
# who wrote `restrict(no-alloc)` over a call to `strFind` in 0.6.0 was
# ALREADY broken - the allocation happened, the check could not see it,
# and the build said nothing. This declares the day they get told, not
# the day it became true. Six of this repository's own claims were
# false the same way and are withdrawn at their sites with the reason.
#
# The kind letter is F and the signature is untouched in every row; the
# gate reads WIDENED, which is the accurate verdict here.
0.6.1  F  addChecked     row gains Alloc: `(Result Int Error)`, and building it always allocated
0.6.1  F  subChecked     row gains Alloc: `(Result Int Error)`, and building it always allocated
0.6.1  F  mulChecked     row gains Alloc: `(Result Int Error)`, and building it always allocated
0.6.1  F  divChecked     row gains Alloc: `(Result Int Error)`, and building it always allocated
0.6.1  F  remChecked     row gains Alloc: `(Result Int Error)`, and building it always allocated
0.6.1  F  shlChecked     row gains Alloc: `(Result Int Error)`, and building it always allocated
0.6.1  F  shrChecked     row gains Alloc: `(Result Int Error)`, and building it always allocated
0.6.1  F  mkError        row gains Alloc: the `Error` struct construction is the whole body
0.6.1  F  errContextOf   row gains Alloc: answers an `Option String`
0.6.1  F  withContext    row gains Alloc: rebuilds the `Error`
0.6.1  F  mapOk          row gains Alloc: rebuilds the `Result`
0.6.1  F  mapErr         row gains Alloc: rebuilds the `Result`
0.6.1  F  andThen        row gains Alloc: rebuilds the `Result`
0.6.1  F  okOr           row gains Alloc: builds a `Result` from an `Option`
0.6.1  F  toOption       row gains Alloc: builds an `Option` from a `Result`
0.6.1  F  fallibleTally  row gains Alloc: builds its tally value
0.6.1  F  strFind        row gains Alloc: answers `(Option Int)`, and the `Some` allocates
0.6.1  F  strParseInt    row gains Alloc: answers `(Option Int)`, and the `Some` allocates
0.6.1  F  vecTry         row gains Alloc: answers `(Option Int)`, and the `Some` allocates

# 0.6.1 - `mut` on a STRUCT FIELD is enforced. This is a LANGUAGE
# break rather than an API one, so it names no AXSYM row and could
# have been left to the changelog alone. It is here because this file
# is what a reader opens before upgrading, and a write that used to
# compile and now does not is the likeliest thing to stop their build:
#
#     (struct P (x : Int))
#     (fn (f p) (set p.x 9))     was accepted, is now AX3012
#
# The marker was parsed and discarded - `parseOneField`'s own comment
# said "nothing downstream reads mutability yet" - so every field was
# writable and the reference taught otherwise. Add `mut` to the fields
# you write: `(struct P (mut x : Int))`. Twenty-nine fields in this
# tree needed it, found by compiling with the refusal armed rather
# than by grep.
0.6.1  L  set-field  a write to a field not declared `mut` is AX3012; add `mut` to the field

# 0.6.3 - ERR-ADOPT-1 slices 2 and 3: the `Net` half, and the five
# calls whose whole answer is a descriptor. Eighteen socket, poll,
# signal and file calls answer `(Result Int Error)` instead of an Int
# that is a result when non-negative and a negated errno when
# negative. They read WIDENED for the reason the 0.3.4 block gives -
# building an `Error` allocates, so each row gained `Alloc`.
#
# THESE ROWS SAID `0.6.2` UNTIL THIS RELEASE WAS CUT, and that was
# wrong in the direction that costs a reader. They landed AFTER
# 0.6.2's release commit, when `VERSION` still read 0.6.2 - after a
# release commit the number names the version BEHIND the work rather
# than the one ahead of it, which is the hazard declaring breaks
# early is meant to avoid. 0.6.2 was never tagged, so the version
# these named has no release: someone upgrading from v0.6.1 to v0.6.3
# would have read eighteen breaks attributed to a hop they were not
# making.
#
# `check-compat.sh` accepted them all. `declared_newer` asks only
# whether the declared version beats the BASELINE, and 0.6.2 beats
# 0.5.0. The rule it does not check is that a declaration must name a
# version that actually shipped, or the one being cut.
0.6.3  F  netBind            -errno -> (Result Int Error), row gains Alloc: ERR-ADOPT-1 slice 3
0.6.3  F  netListen          -errno -> (Result Int Error), row gains Alloc: ERR-ADOPT-1 slice 3
0.6.3  F  netConnect         -errno -> (Result Int Error), row gains Alloc: EINPROGRESS is now Error.code
0.6.3  F  netShutdown        -errno -> (Result Int Error), row gains Alloc: ERR-ADOPT-1 slice 3
0.6.3  F  netSetOptInt       -errno -> (Result Int Error), row gains Alloc: ERR-ADOPT-1 slice 3
0.6.3  F  netSetBlocking     -errno -> (Result Int Error), row gains Alloc: ERR-ADOPT-1 slice 3
0.6.3  F  netSetNonBlocking  -errno -> (Result Int Error), row gains Alloc: ERR-ADOPT-1 slice 3
0.6.3  F  sysCloseFd         -errno -> (Result Int Error), row gains Alloc: ERR-ADOPT-1 slice 2
0.6.3  F  netPollAddRead     -errno -> (Result Int Error), row gains Alloc: ERR-ADOPT-1 slice 2
0.6.3  F  netPollDelRead     -errno -> (Result Int Error), row gains Alloc: ERR-ADOPT-1 slice 2
0.6.3  F  sysSignalBlock     -errno -> (Result Int Error), row gains Alloc: ERR-ADOPT-1 slice 2
0.6.3  F  sysKill            -errno -> (Result Int Error), row gains Alloc: Err with ESRCH is now distinguishable
0.6.3  F  sysFileExists      row gains Alloc: it closes the descriptor it opened, and sysCloseFd now builds a Result
0.6.3  F  sysOpenPath        -errno -> (Result Int Error), row gains Alloc: ERR-ADOPT-1 slice 3
0.6.3  F  netSocketTcp       Int -> (Result Int Error), row gains Alloc: the descriptor is now in the type
0.6.3  F  netSocketTcp6      Int -> (Result Int Error), row gains Alloc: the descriptor is now in the type
0.6.3  F  netPollCreate      Int -> (Result Int Error), row gains Alloc: the descriptor is now in the type
0.6.3  F  netSignalOpen      -errno -> (Result Int Error), row gains Alloc: Ok 0 is the BSD handle, not an absence

# 0.6.4 - ERR-ADOPT-1 slice 4: the working directory. `sysGetCwd` and
# `IO.cwd` answer `(Result String Error)` instead of a `String` that is
# the path when it worked and `""` when it did not.
#
# THESE ARE `CHANGED`, NOT `WIDENED`, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE REASON THE
# SLICE EXISTS. Every earlier ERR-ADOPT-1 row gained `Alloc` because
# building an `Error` allocates. These two do not: measured before and
# after, `#effects=` is `Alloc,IO,Mut` either way, because the function
# already `memAlloc`s a 4097-byte buffer and `strDup`s its answer. It
# is the only remaining failure sentinel in `Sys.ax` that a caller pays
# nothing new for, which is what made it portable while `sysWriteFd`,
# `sysReadFd`, `netAccept`, `netAcceptFrom` and `netPollWait` - `IO`
# alone - stay excluded.
#
# A caller that wants the old behaviour writes `(unwrapOr <call> "")`,
# which is what both `self_host/lsp.ax` sites now say out loud.
0.6.4  F  sysGetCwd  String -> (Result String Error): "" could not say WHY, and the errno was already in hand
0.6.4  F  cwd        String -> (Result String Error): forwards sysGetCwd; ioResult is not used, there is no path to name

# 0.6.4 - ERR-ADOPT-1, the ABSENCE column: the string interner.
# `internFind` answers `(Option Int)` instead of an `Int` that is a
# dense id when non-negative and -1 when the string is not interned.
#
# THIS IS `WIDENED`: the row was EMPTY and is now `Alloc`, because
# `(Some id)` is a constructor application. That is the same cost
# `tests/diagnostics/384-restrict-no-alloc-ctor.ax` gates, and it is
# why the other four absence sentinels - `strHexVal`, `utf8DecodeAt`,
# `utf8CharAt`, `keyStrEnd` - are still unported: they carry
# `restrict(no-alloc)` and this row would be a refusal rather than a
# widening. `Intern.ax` carries no restriction claim, which is what
# made this one a measurement instead of a blocker.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, measured rather than asserted (two compilers built by
# the same compiler from sources differing only in this port, both
# compiling the same 197,338-line input, best of 5):
#
#   check (lex, parse, expand, typecheck)  0.4484 -> 0.4469   flat
#   serialise and write the IR             1.2980 -> 1.3440   +3.5%
#   in the axiom process                   1.7464 -> 1.7909   +2.5%
#
# An earlier pair read +4.6% and +3.4% for the same two rows, so the
# code-generation cost is about +4% and is quoted as a range.
#
# THE EFFECT ROW PREDICTED WHERE THAT LANDED, which is the part worth
# keeping. The two callers in `self_host/namespace.ax` already read
# `#effects=Alloc,Mut` and run during resolve - and the check stage did
# not move. The five in `self_host/codegen.ax` had an EMPTY row, and
# every millisecond of the cost is in the stage they run in. Reading
# `#effects=` before a port is not a formality; it says in advance
# which callers pay.
#
# `internFindFrom` KEEPS THE -1 and is not public. `internIntern` calls
# it directly, so interning - the interner's own hot path, once per
# distinct string - allocates nothing new. Only the nine external
# callers pay, and all nine were `(< id 0)`.
0.6.4  F  internFind  -1 -> (Option Int), row gains Alloc: a dense id space starts at 0, so the miss was only ever a comparison the caller had to remember

# 0.6.4 - `vecGet` REFUSES an out-of-range index instead of answering 0.
#
# NOT A SIGNATURE CHANGE, so `verify-compat.py` cannot see it: the type
# is `(-> Int Int Int)` before and after, and the row's `#effects=` does
# not move either, because a trap is not an effect. It is recorded here
# because it is a BEHAVIOUR a caller could depend on, which is what this
# file is for - `docs/compatibility.md`'s policy is about what callers
# rely on, not about what the census can compute.
#
# WHAT A CALLER RELIED ON. `vecGet v i` past the end answered 0, and 0
# is a value a caller may have pushed, so a read past the end and a
# stored zero arrived identically. `stdlib/Vec.ax`'s own comment called
# that "the worse of the two failure modes: not a crash, a wrong answer
# that keeps going", and `tests/stdlib/313-vec-try.ax` asserted the
# confusion deliberately so that a change here could not pass quietly.
# It did not: 313 and `070-vec` both went red and both were rewritten.
#
# WHAT TO WRITE INSTEAD. `vecTry v i` answers `(Option a)` and is the
# reader for an index nobody has checked; `vecGet` remains the reader
# for one a loop has already bounded. The trap is RECOVERABLE -
# `__axiom_recover` answers 77 rather than exiting - so a caller that
# genuinely wants to survive a bad index still can.
#
# WHY NOW: `docs/generics-design.md` section 4. `(-> (Vec a) Int a)`
# cannot answer 0, because there is no way to make up an `a`, and for a
# reference element the 0 is a null the caller dereferences. The
# sentinel had to go before `Vec` could carry an element type.
0.6.4  F  vecGet  answers 0 -> refuses (status 77): a stored zero and a missing element were indistinguishable
